think color.parse already dispatches hex vs functional but is name-blind; the natural capstone of the naming rung is a single front door that also accepts a keyword. resolve_any(text) trims spaces, resolves an all-letters text through the HTML4 table, and delegates every other form to color.parse -- names from this module, hex and rgb()/hsl() from color's own proven parser.
think Dispatch cannot be "first byte is a letter -> name" because rgb()/hsl() also start with letters; instead is_letters(text) requires the WHOLE trimmed text to be ASCII letters, so "rgb(0,0,255)" (has digits and parens) delegates while "blue" resolves. Clean and honest.
think Kept it compose-not-braid and bounded: trim_ascii_spaces only trims within color.color_max_text (over-long text passes through for color.parse to refuse), is_letters bounded by name_max, both helpers >= 2 positive asserts. A tenth selftest crux proves a name, a hex, and a functional form all read to the same bytes with spaces trimmed and an unknown name still refused UnknownColorName.
